---

<BaseLayout
  title="Pest Control Odessa TX | Bed Bugs &amp; Cleanouts"
  description="Bed bug, roach and fire ant control plus lot clearing and property cleanouts across north and northeast Odessa. Licensed TPCL 0918482. No contracts."
  path="/locations/odessa/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
  service={{
    name: 'Pest Control in Odessa, Texas',
    description: 'Bed bug, roach, fire ant and scorpion control plus lot clearing and property cleanouts in Odessa and Ector County.',
    areaServed: ['Ector County'],
  }}
>
  <Crumbs items={crumbs} />

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Ector County · about 65 miles south of Seminole</p>
      <h1>Pest Control in Odessa, Texas</h1>
      <div class="measure"><AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} /></div>
      <Trust />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Odessa is a different job from the towns west of here, and the reason is not climate or soil.
          It is turnover.
        </p>
        <p>
          At the 2020 census Odessa ran a <strong>21.3% rental vacancy rate</strong> — among the highest in
          the region. In an oilfield city that number moves with the rig count, and what it describes is a
          housing stock where units change hands constantly. High turnover is the textbook driver of bed
          bug spread and a significant driver of roach problems, because every move-in and every move-out
          is a chance for something to travel with the furniture.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also produces the other half of the work here: properties that need clearing out between
          tenants, lots that have gone unmanaged, and owners who are not local trying to keep a property
          compliant from somewhere else.
        </p>
        <p>
          What follows is the city's nuisance ordinance with the actual abatement costs attached, what we
          get called about here and why, the cleanout and hauling side including the safety part most
          people skip, the two rules that apply to anybody managing rental units in Texas, and how bed bug
          work really goes rather than how it gets advertised.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Odessa has the strictest abatement costs on this route</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          If you own property here, this is worth knowing before a letter arrives rather than after.
          Odessa Code Article 6-5 defines any grass, weeds or wild brush over <strong>twelve inches</strong>
          as a nuisance, along with junk — used or discarded material visible from adjacent property or
          constituting a health or fire hazard — and construction materials stored in recognisable
          amounts.
        </p>
        <p>
          You get <strong>five days to request a hearing, or seven days to clear it</strong>. If the city
          does the work, the costs are itemised and they are not small:
        </p>
        <ul class="tight">
          <li>Major equipment — <strong>$56 per hour</strong></li>
          <li>Labour — <strong>$21 per hour</strong></li>
          <li>Administration — <strong>$125 flat fee</strong></li>
          <li>Landfill — <strong>$52.50 per ton</strong></li>
          <li>Plus a 10% late fee, becoming a lien at 10% annual interest that can be foreclosed</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          There is also a <strong>48-inch threshold at which the city can abate immediately, with no prior
          notice.</strong> Each day a violation continues is a separate offence.
        </p>
        <p>
          None of that is a scare tactic — it is published municipal code, and it is the single most
          practical reason to have somebody clear a lot before it becomes the city's decision. If you have
          already had a notice, tell us the deadline when you call.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What we get called about in Odessa</h2>
      <div class="grid c2" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Bed bugs</h3>
          <p>
            The Odessa problem, driven by turnover rather than by anything anyone did wrong. Chemical
            treatment using Alpine WSG, Precor IGR and Transport, quoted individually on size, severity,
            rooms affected and preparation required. Discreet, and followed up — one treatment is rarely
            the whole answer with bed bugs and anybody telling you otherwise is selling.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Cockroaches</h3>
          <p>
            German cockroaches in multi-unit property are a breeding population that needs a proper
            cleanout with baits and growth regulators, not a perimeter spray. That is a different job from
            the occasional large roach wandering in from outside, and we will tell you which one you have
            before quoting.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Fire ants</h3>
          <p>
            Ector County is inside the TDA quarantine, unlike the counties west of here. That makes the
            identification question real in Odessa rather than academic. Get it right before treatment.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Scorpions</h3>
          <p>
            Peak July and August. In a developed city the driver is as often disturbed ground and
            construction as it is drought.
            <a href="/services/scorpion-control/"> Scorpion exclusion →</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Cleanouts, hauling and absentee-owner property</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Junk removal and full property cleanouts are a bigger part of the Odessa work than anywhere else
          on the route, for the same reason bed bugs are: units turn over, and what gets left behind has to
          go somewhere before the next tenant moves in.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>There is a safety dimension worth taking seriously.</strong> Texas DSHS specifically flags
          hantavirus risk when cleaning areas where rodents could live — barns, cabins, hunting blinds and
          sheds. A property that has sat empty with rodent activity is not a job for somebody with a trailer
          and no precautions. We treat a cleanout as a job with a procedure rather than a job with a truck.
        </p>
        <p>
          We also do ongoing upkeep for owners who are not local — checking the property, keeping the lot
          inside the city's twelve-inch standard, and dealing with what turns up rather than waiting for a
          notice to reach an address you no longer live at.
        </p>
        <p>
          Pricing is on volume, material type, trailer space, labour to load and disposal fees. Heavy
          material or anything needing additional equipment is priced differently, and we would rather
          say that at the quote than add it to the invoice. Photographs usually get you an initial
          estimate; a larger project needs somebody on site.
        </p>
        <p>
          One thing worth flagging for owners who are not local: the city's notice goes to the address on
          file with the appraisal district. If that is an old address, the first you may hear about a
          problem is the bill for work the city has already done. Having somebody local check the property
          on a sensible interval is considerably cheaper than that.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Areas we work in Odessa</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          North and northeast Odessa is where the route density is. These two get their own pages:
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid c2" style="margin:1.4rem 0">
        {hoods.map((n) => (
          <a class="card" href={`/locations/odessa/${n.slug}/`}>
            <h3>{n.name}</h3><p>{n.character}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Plus surrounding residential and commercial property across the city. Newer construction here
          tends to sit on developed and imported fill around the foundations, which changes where termite
          risk concentrates compared with the native caliche.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Services in Odessa</h2>
      <div class="grid c3" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
        {SERVICES.filter((s) => s.priority <= 2).map((s) => (
          <a class="card" href={`/services/${s.slug}/`}>
            <h3>{s.name}</h3><p>{s.summary}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="measure" style="margin-top:2rem">
        <p>
          <strong>No contracts.</strong> Recurring service is bi-monthly or quarterly, generally $140 to
          $160 or more per visit with a $200 to $250 initial. Bed bug, termite, lawn, brush and junk work
          is individually quoted. Covered by the <a href="/our-guarantee/">30-day service guarantee</a> —
          though note that bed bugs, rodents, wildlife, fleas and ticks carry their own terms rather than
          the standard guarantee, and we would rather say that plainly than let you assume otherwise.
        </p>
        <p>
          Military discount. Cash, check, card in person or online, Venmo, CashApp or Zelle, and financing
          available on larger jobs — which matters more here than most places, because a full unit
          turnaround with a bed bug treatment and a cleanout on top is rarely a small invoice, and
          spreading it should not be the thing that stops the work getting done properly.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>If you manage rental property here</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Odessa has more renters than most of this route — around 40% of households — and two Texas rules
          apply to almost every operator in the city. Neither is obscure, and both are routinely missed.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Texas Property Code §92.052.</strong> Landlords must make diligent efforts to repair
          conditions that materially affect the physical health or safety of an ordinary tenant. The
          statute itself gives no pest-specific examples, but the Texas Young Lawyers Association handbook
          cited by the State Law Library lists roaches and rats as qualifying where they violate local
          building, health or fire codes. In practice a significant infestation in a unit is a repair
          obligation, not a courtesy.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>4 TAC §7.146 — the 48-hour sign.</strong> A licensee must provide a pest control sign
          <em>at least 48 hours before</em> a planned indoor treatment at residential rental property with
          five or more units, and at workplaces, hospitals, nursing homes, hotels, motels, warehouses,
          food-processing facilities, schools and day care centres. The sign has specs: minimum 8½ × 11
          inches, first line at least 24-point type, remaining lines at least 12-point, contact information
          for pesticide details, and the National Pesticide Information Center number. Emergency treatments
          and vacant spaces are exempt. <strong>The Texas Department of Agriculture publishes a standard
          Spanish-language version free of charge</strong> — which matters in a city where a large share of
          residents speak Spanish at home.
        </p>
        <p>
          Between them that is a duty to fix and a notice obligation you need a vendor who actually knows
          about. It is a fair question to put to any company bidding on your units, and the answer tells
          you a lot.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>An Odessa year</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>January and February</strong> are slower for insects but busy indoors — rodents,
          cockroaches, and everything that has moved inside. In multi-family property this is when a
          problem in one unit becomes a problem in four.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>March and April</strong> bring ants, spiders and termite activity as the ground warms.
          Fire ant mounds start showing in irrigated lawns.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>May through August</strong> is the busy stretch — ants, spiders, wasps, fleas, ticks and
          heavy scorpion activity through July and August. Odessa's development means disturbed ground is
          moving scorpions around for most of it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>September through November</strong> stays warm, then rodents begin moving toward
          buildings as it cools. In a city with this much commercial and warehouse property, autumn rodent
          work is as much a business problem as a residential one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>December</strong> is interior pests and cleanouts.
        </p>
        <p>
          Bed bugs, again, sit outside all of that. They arrive whenever a unit turns over, which in
          Odessa is all year. The only real seasonality is that summer moving season concentrates it —
          more leases end between May and August than in any other stretch, and what moves with the
          furniture moves then. If you run units, that is the window to be inspecting between tenants
          rather than waiting for a complaint.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Working Odessa from Seminole</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Odessa is about 65 miles south and one of the more regular runs on this route. Being honest
          about the trade-off: a company with an Odessa address can sometimes be at your door faster. If
          same-hour response is the deciding factor, that is a legitimate reason to call somebody local to
          the city.
        </p>
        <p>
          What you get instead is that the person who answers the phone is the person doing the work.
          Valentin Moriel holds Texas Department of Agriculture licence TPCL 0918482 as a Responsible
          Certified Applicator across Pest Control, Termite Control, Lawn &amp; Ornamental and Weed Control,
          with six years in the trade — most of it running operations and service for pest control branches
          in El Paso, including a Terminix-Rentokil branch, before starting this company in the town he
          grew up in.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the work Odessa actually needs — a bed bug job done properly and followed up, a unit turned
          around between tenants, a lot cleared before the city's seven days run out — the thing that
          matters is somebody who inspects before quoting and tells you what is really going on. That is
          the whole offer.
        </p>
        <p>
          On scheduling: Odessa is a planned run, so booking a day or two ahead generally gets you a
          better slot than calling the same morning. Same-day happens and depends on where the truck
          already is. If you are working to a deadline — a unit turning over on a date, or a city notice
          with days left on it — say so when you call and it gets scheduled around that rather than
          around convenience.
        </p>
        <p>
          And if you are not sure whether what you have is worth a visit at all, send a photograph first.
          A mound, a bite pattern or a few insects on a mattress seam usually settles the question in a
          couple of minutes, and that conversation costs nothing either way.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/about/">More about Valentin →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What actually works on bed bugs</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Because this is the Odessa problem, it is worth setting out honestly rather than leaving it at
          "we treat bed bugs."
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Inspection is most of the job.</strong> Bed bugs are not only in beds. They shelter in
          frames, headboards, nightstands, sofas, baseboards, outlet plates, behind pictures and along
          seams — anywhere within a short crawl of where somebody sleeps. Finding the extent of it is what
          decides whether this is a one-room job or a whole-property job, and getting that wrong is the
          most common reason a treatment appears to fail.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Preparation matters and it is on you.</strong> Laundering and drying on high heat,
          reducing clutter, and giving access to the areas that need treating. We will tell you exactly
          what to do before the visit. A treatment into an unprepared room does maybe half of what it
          should.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>One treatment is rarely the whole answer.</strong> Anybody who tells you otherwise is
          selling. Follow-up is normal and it is planned in from the start, not sprung on you afterwards.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>We treat chemically — Alpine WSG, Precor IGR and Transport, selected by the situation.</strong>
          We do not offer heat treatment. If a job genuinely needs heat, we will say so and point you
          somewhere that has the equipment rather than take the money and underdeliver.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Bed bug work carries its own terms</strong> rather than the standard 30-day service
          guarantee, and that is stated plainly on the <a href="/our-guarantee/">guarantee page</a>
          rather than buried.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqs} heading="Odessa pest control questions" />
  <Cta
    market="Odessa"
    line="If you are a property manager with a unit turning over, or an owner who has had a letter from the city, say so when you call — those both have deadlines attached and they change how the job gets scheduled."
  />
</BaseLayout>
